groups:
- number: '01'
  heading: Cost factors
  faqs:
  - q: What does a sports surface actually cost?
    a_paragraphs:
    - Cost per square metre varies by a factor of five across surfaces, so any number without a specification attached is meaningless. An acrylic pickleball court and a full-PU athletics track are not comparable.
    - 'What we can tell you: on a raw-soil site, expect roughly 55–70% of the total to be civil work and drainage, not the surface. Quotes that are cheap usually cut there.'
  - q: Why are two quotes for "the same turf" so far apart?
    a_paragraphs:
    - Almost always the base. One quote includes excavation, graded sub-base, compaction testing and drainage; the other assumes your ground is ready and will raise a variation once work starts.
    - Compare the build-up section, not the headline. Ask both contractors for pile height, dtex, shock pad thickness and sub-base depth in writing.
  - q: Can we phase the work to spread the cost?
    a_paragraphs:
    - Yes, and it is often sensible. Base and drainage in one financial year, surface in the next. A properly built base can sit exposed for months.
    - What we advise against is phasing the surface itself — a half-coated court wears unevenly and the joint always shows.
  - q: Do you work with school and government budgets?
    a_paragraphs:
    - Yes. We quote against tender specifications and can provide the itemised BOQ format most institutions require. We will also flag where a tender specification is internally contradictory before you publish it.
- number: '02'
  heading: Timelines
  faqs:
  - q: How long from order to first game?
    a_paragraphs:
    - 'On a prepared, level base: two to three weeks for most courts. On raw ground: five to eight weeks, because excavation, sub-base and curing cannot be rushed.'
    - An athletics track or a full-size football pitch runs longer — eight to fourteen weeks depending on site access.
  - q: Does the monsoon stop work?
    a_paragraphs:
    - It stops civil work and acrylic coating; it does not stop turf laying or indoor installation. We schedule base work outside the monsoon window and use the wet weeks for indoor jobs.
    - If your deadline is fixed, tell us early — sequence matters more than crew size.
  - q: How much notice do you need to start?
    a_paragraphs:
    - Typically two to three weeks from a signed order, longer in October to February when demand peaks. Imported turf and vinyl can carry a four to six week lead time, so we confirm stock before committing to a date.
- number: '03'
  heading: Durability
  faqs:
  - q: How many years will the surface last?
    a_paragraphs:
    - 'Artificial turf: eight to ten years recreational, six to seven under heavy academy use. Acrylic: recoat every four to six years, base lasts decades. Indoor vinyl: ten to fifteen years. Wooden sprung floor: twenty-five years or more with refinishing.'
    - The base beneath all of them outlives two surface replacements if it was built correctly.
  - q: What fails first, and why?
    a_paragraphs:
    - Drainage. Water that cannot leave sits under the surface, softens the base and lifts seams or delaminates coating. Nearly every surface failure we are called to inspect is a water problem wearing a material costume.
  - q: Will the colour fade?
    a_paragraphs:
    - Some fade is normal and covered by the published UV figure. Good monofilament yarn and pigmented acrylic hold colour for years; cheap yarn goes grey in two summers, which is why we name the dtex and the warranty on the quote.
- number: '04'
  heading: Maintenance
  faqs:
  - q: How much maintenance does it really need?
    a_paragraphs:
    - Less than natural grass, more than nothing. Turf needs weekly brushing and an annual infill top-up. Acrylic needs sweeping and a recoat every few years. Indoor vinyl needs daily dry mopping and never wax.
    - Full detail, plus AMC tiers, is on the <a href="/maintenance/">Maintenance page</a>.
  - q: Is an AMC compulsory?
    a_paragraphs:
    - Not with us. But most surface warranties — ours and the manufacturers' — require documented annual maintenance. You can do it yourself and keep records, or contract it to us. What voids a claim is having neither.
  - q: Can you maintain a surface another contractor built?
    a_paragraphs:
    - Yes, and we do it often. We inspect first and give you a written condition report before quoting, including telling you if the surface is beyond economic maintenance.
cta_heading: Still Have a Question?
cta_text: WhatsApp it to us with a photograph of your site. You will get an answer, not a brochure.
